    "requirements": [
      {
        "requirement": "Caddy that holds two jerry cans of petrol upright",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "Built a candidate: two 3-storey hex cells (piece:0,0 and piece:1,0, fused stacks) each with a 3-storey-deep full bin (bin:0,0,2 and bin:1,0,2), joint bolts on the first storey. Overall about 334 x 249 x 252 mm, meshClash clear, no rule errors. But the configurator has no jerry-can part or prop, so I could not verify that a can fits upright. The bins are about 190 mm hex cells and 240 mm deep. A typical 20 L can is roughly 390 mm tall and about 300 mm wide, so it would likely stand out of the top of the bin and be too wide for one cell. Treat this as a layout sketch, not a verified holder."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Holds the cans securely in a car boot (restraint in a crash or on the move)",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "No strap, clamp or lid retention was added, and the configurator does not model vehicle loads. The joint bolts only lock the two stacks together."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Printed material is suitable for petrol (fuel resistance, durability)",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "The configurator does not model material, fuel compatibility or chemical resistance. This must be checked for the material you choose to print in."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Fits in the boot of the user's car",
        "status": "unmet",
        "evidence": "Car boot dimensions were not provided or modelled. The caddy is about 334 x 252 mm footprint and 249 mm tall, which would need checking against the actual boot."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "I built a two-cell caddy sketch (two tall hex stacks with 3-storey full bins, joint-bolted, clear of mesh clashes), but it cannot be shown to hold two upright petrol jerry cans. The configurator has no can geometry, and a standard 20 L can is taller and wider than the bins appear to allow. Before you print, measure your own cans and boot, and confirm the material resists petrol. Also, petrol vapour and spills in an enclosed boot are a real hazard: a caddy like this should be well ventilated and secured, and fuel is best carried in the boot only if the cans are sealed, upright and restrained, with the boot ventilated and no fuel kept in the cabin.\"}"
  },
